(NULL, 'mc', 'In gaming, what does "RPG" stand for?', 'pop_culture', '11_12', 'medium', true, '[{"label":"Rapid Play Game","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Role Playing Game","isCorrect":true},{"label":"Real Player Gaming","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Ranked Position Game","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'RPG stands for Role Playing Game — a genre where players take on the role of a character and make choices that affect the story, like Final Fantasy or Skyrim.'),
(NULL, 'mc', 'Which author created the fictional detective Sherlock Holmes?', 'pop_culture', '11_12', 'medium', true, '[{"label":"Agatha Christie","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Edgar Allan Poe","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Arthur Conan Doyle","isCorrect":true},{"label":"G.K. Chesterton","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Sir Arthur Conan Doyle created Sherlock Holmes in 1887. Holmes first appeared in "A Study in Scarlet" and became one of fiction''s most famous detectives.'),

-- age_band: 11_12 | difficulty: hard
(NULL, 'mc', 'What literary device is used when an author gives hints about what will happen later in the story?', 'pop_culture', '11_12', 'hard', true, '[{"label":"Allegory","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Irony","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Foreshadowing","isCorrect":true},{"label":"Anachronism","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Foreshadowing is when an author plants subtle clues about future events. Shakespeare used it constantly — for example, when the witches hint at Macbeth''s fate early on.'),
(NULL, 'mc', 'The film "2001: A Space Odyssey" was directed by which filmmaker?', 'pop_culture', '11_12', 'hard', true, '[{"label":"Steven Spielberg","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Stanley Kubrick","isCorrect":true},{"label":"Ridley Scott","isCorrect":false},{"label":"George Lucas","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Stanley Kubrick directed "2001: A Space Odyssey" (1968), a groundbreaking sci-fi film renowned for its realistic depiction of space and its mysterious ending.'),
(NULL, 'mc', 'In speedrunning, what does "any%" mean?', 'pop_culture', '11_12', 'hard', true, '[{"label":"Completing the game with 100% of items","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Finishing the game as fast as possible using any means","isCorrect":true},{"label":"Winning without taking any damage","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Collecting any percentage of collectibles","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, '"Any%" speedruns mean finishing the game as fast as possible using any glitches or shortcuts available — the goal is simply to reach the credits in the shortest time.'),
(NULL, 'mc', 'In "Animal Farm" by George Orwell, the pigs taking control of the farm is an allegory for what real historical event?', 'pop_culture', '11_12', 'hard', true, '[{"label":"World War I","isCorrect":false},{"label":"The French Revolution","isCorrect":false},{"label":"The Russian Revolution","isCorrect":true},{"label":"The American Civil War","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, '"Animal Farm" is an allegory for the Russian Revolution and the rise of Stalinism. Napoleon the pig represents Joseph Stalin, and the farm represents Soviet Russia.'),
(NULL, 'mc', 'What is the term for when a film breaks the fourth wall and a character speaks directly to the audience?', 'pop_culture', '11_12', 'hard', true, '[{"label":"Soliloquy","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Direct address","isCorrect":true},{"label":"Aside","isCorrect":false},{"label":"Monologue","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'Direct address (also called breaking the fourth wall) is when a character looks at the camera and speaks to the audience — used memorably in "Ferris Bueller''s Day Off" and "Deadpool."'),

